1
00:00:11,420 --> 00:00:13,500
That's easy. It's a boss.

2
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:21,020
Every manager I know finds managing his boss the most difficult of his tasks.

3
00:00:21,810 --> 00:00:23,550
Very few managers even try.

4
00:00:24,090 --> 00:00:36,820
Well, I think this is one of the few kind of dependable symptoms

5
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:42,660
as to which of the younger people in your organization are going to go places

6
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:45,460
and which ones are going to go nowhere.

7
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:52,100
the ones who only talk about how they suffer from the boss

8
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:55,760
and how incompetent he is and how impossible he is.

9
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:58,400
Well, they are not going to go any place.

10
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:05,510
One can spot the comers because they do something about managing the boss,

11
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:08,360
and it's really quite simple.

12
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:17,850
When you talk about something different,

13
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:21,160
which polishing the apple is, so to speak,

14
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:24,800
a subordinate clause rather than the main one.

15
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:27,160
It almost depends on the boss what you do.

16
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:32,520
The first thing to know is that the boss is a human being,

17
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:37,680
perversely insisting on behaving like a human being,

18
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:40,240
neither a monster nor an angel,

19
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:46,480
and therefore he has to be treated as a human being and as an individual.

20
00:01:48,170 --> 00:01:50,270
And some you polish the apple for,

21
00:01:50,490 --> 00:01:53,270
and others that's the worst thing you could possibly do.

22
00:01:53,270 --> 00:02:00,730
The second thing is that no matter how able and competent he is,

23
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:03,110
he is not the mind reader.

24
00:02:04,130 --> 00:02:10,690
And therefore, you have to make sure that he understands what you're trying to do.

25
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:17,070
And the third thing is that sure, he does not give you enough of his time,

26
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:23,060
but he gives you more time as a rule than he should and more than he has.

27
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:29,020
and it's up to you to make sure that you get the right amount of time

28
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:30,860
and that it's productive time.

29
00:02:31,770 --> 00:02:39,020
Finally, it's more dangerous to underrate the boss than to overrate him.

30
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:45,790
The most serious mistake you can make is to underrate the boss

31
00:02:45,790 --> 00:02:47,910
and be caught doing it.

32
00:02:48,370 --> 00:02:52,370
Those are kind of very simple, stupid rules.

33
00:02:52,370 --> 00:02:58,750
very few managers know them, and even fewer do something about them.

34
00:03:03,390 --> 00:03:04,110
How do all this?

35
00:03:04,350 --> 00:03:10,230
I'm always amazed just to be very primitive, but these are primitive things.

36
00:03:10,610 --> 00:03:15,410
I'm always amazed by the fact that almost nobody seems to know

37
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:19,730
that the human race is divided into readers and listeners

38
00:03:19,730 --> 00:03:22,970
the way it's divided into right-handers and left-handers,

39
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:27,330
and that the first thing you have to do is look at your boss and say,

40
00:03:27,330 --> 00:03:30,610
how does he function, what makes him tick.

41
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:33,310
If you have a reader for a boss,

42
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:37,190
let's say his name is Dwight Eisenhower,

43
00:03:38,510 --> 00:03:41,350
because he was a perfect example of a reader,

44
00:03:41,430 --> 00:03:43,070
or John Kennedy, another reader.

45
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:45,050
You don't go and talk to him.

46
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:50,000
You make sure he can read first, then you can talk.

47
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:51,640
If you have a listener,

48
00:03:53,090 --> 00:03:57,440
and let's say his name is Franklin Roosevelt or Harry Truman,

49
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:01,230
you don't send in a memorandum first.

50
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:04,830
You go in and talk first, and you can leave a memorandum.

51
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:08,900
If you have a man who likes to be flattered

52
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:11,980
because he needs a little reassurance,

53
00:04:12,540 --> 00:04:14,680
well, then you see a few nice things.

54
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:19,880
And if you notice a man is embarrassed, as I am, for instance,

55
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:23,620
one of the things I dislike the most is when people tell me

56
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:25,160
about how good my last book is.

57
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:27,780
It's stupid. It embarrasses me.

58
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:30,520
You have a boss who likes to be flattered.

59
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:31,940
All right, it's cheap.

60
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:36,800
You have a boss who has to have facts and figures,

61
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:39,640
an old financial man, again, like myself.

62
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:44,780
Well, then you just lift 150 pages out of the statistical abstract.

63
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:49,240
You have a boss who wants one page of recommendations.

64
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:50,700
Well, that's what he gets.

65
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:55,260
He accepts the fact that he's a human being,

66
00:04:56,080 --> 00:05:01,440
that he's unlikely to be changed by you, set in his ways,

67
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:04,380
and that it's up to you to make him function.

68
00:05:04,860 --> 00:05:06,980
People who want to manage their boss

69
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:11,120
first find out whom it is they want to manage.

70
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:13,260
One doesn't manage a boss,

71
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:15,480
one works with a human being.

72
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:25,240
I don't know about generally,

73
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:28,280
but your batting average is going to be a great deal better.

74
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:31,620
This may strike you as perfectly trivial, Elizabeth.

75
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:36,710
Learn also the good words and the bad words.

76
00:05:36,710 --> 00:05:42,720
I once worked with perhaps the ablest man among all my clients.

77
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:46,160
We all had to learn never to say control.

78
00:05:47,790 --> 00:05:50,910
He was an engineer and an autocrat and a pedant,

79
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:55,220
but control was a bad, bad, bad word.

80
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:58,220
We had to learn to say measurement.

81
00:05:59,010 --> 00:06:03,470
If you said control, his mind just snapped shut,

82
00:06:03,870 --> 00:06:05,650
and for the next six weeks,

83
00:06:05,650 --> 00:06:10,110
all he could do is deliver sermons on why control is bad.

84
00:06:11,010 --> 00:06:19,920
everybody has worth magic. It's my job to work with this person as my resource.

85
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:25,120
Resources always have to be worked the way they are, not the way I want them to be.

86
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:32,720
Yes, it's manipulative. It's your job to manipulate in the sense of adapting yourself

87
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:37,220
to the way an individual is put together.

88
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:56,280
them. Well, we don't only assume this about the boss, you know, children assume it about their

89
00:06:56,280 --> 00:07:01,680
parents and parents assume it about their children and teachers about their students and students

90
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:08,240
about their teachers. Apparently, the most difficult thing to accept is that first insight

91
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:15,440
of modern psychology. Modern psychology started around 1880 with the starting discovery that what

92
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:23,430
is obvious to me nobody else can see at all. Very few people accept this. Particularly in dealing

93
00:07:23,430 --> 00:07:30,710
with your boss, you better keep it in mind. It's your job to make sure he knows what you are trying

94
00:07:30,710 --> 00:07:41,770
to do. He knows what you need. He knows what you expect from him. And if you just blithely go ahead

95
00:07:41,770 --> 00:07:44,010
assuming it's so obvious what I'm doing.

96
00:07:44,110 --> 00:07:45,930
Any goddamn fool must see it.

97
00:07:46,550 --> 00:07:48,050
Well, then everybody will see now

98
00:07:48,050 --> 00:07:50,290
what the dickness is that fellow up to.

99
00:07:50,930 --> 00:07:52,270
I can't figure it out.

100
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:54,220
To manage your boss,

101
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:56,560
think through what you're trying to do,

102
00:07:56,940 --> 00:08:00,040
including those things your boss better not know about.

103
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:02,640
In every organization,

104
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:05,180
there's a becoming amount of sabotage,

105
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:07,700
and it's needed, but not too much.

106
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:12,200
But what are the things people have to know?

107
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:16,540
what are you trying to do, what can they expect from you,

108
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:19,200
what are you not going to do,

109
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:24,540
and then make sure that fellow on whom you depend so heavily,

110
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:29,980
the fellow in that office next door who is your boss,

111
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:34,710
understands it, not necessarily approves, but understands it,

112
00:08:34,770 --> 00:08:37,980
so that he isn't surprised.

113
00:08:39,390 --> 00:08:42,630
One doesn't surprise the boss because there are no pleasant surprises.

114
00:08:42,630 --> 00:08:50,620
surprises. One doesn't baffle him. One goes in or one writes to him, depending on how he receives,

115
00:08:51,140 --> 00:08:58,160
and says, this is what I'm trying to do. This is what I am not going to do. I know we have

116
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:04,990
that problem with the branch office in England, and I'm not going to tackle it because I think

117
00:09:04,990 --> 00:09:12,140
that if we leave it alone, they'll work it out. I'll check it. And if they don't work it out in

118
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:15,480
Six months from now, I'll come down on them like a ton of bricks.

119
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:21,920
So that he doesn't say, well, doesn't that fellow see the mess we are in there in the London office?

120
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:24,160
Why doesn't he do anything about it?

121
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:25,860
Is he asleep or what?

122
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:28,410
He knows you have made a decision.

123
00:09:29,930 --> 00:09:34,530
You're giving those English people another six months to straighten it out.

124
00:09:35,510 --> 00:09:38,490
All right, if they don't straighten it out, you'll have to tackle it.

125
00:09:38,650 --> 00:09:39,650
But that's a decision.

126
00:09:40,390 --> 00:09:41,950
Don't say it's obvious.

127
00:09:41,950 --> 00:09:50,130
It is, except the fact that mind readers exist only in science fiction novels.

128
00:09:50,750 --> 00:09:53,150
They do not exist in the executive office.

129
00:10:09,290 --> 00:10:10,530
That depends on the boss.

130
00:10:10,870 --> 00:10:11,290
I suppose.

131
00:10:11,310 --> 00:10:17,220
There are some bosses where one doesn't do it because they don't like it.

132
00:10:17,480 --> 00:10:19,680
But let me say one thing one never does.

133
00:10:21,050 --> 00:10:23,450
One never hides problems.

134
00:10:24,010 --> 00:10:26,790
One never brushes an elephant under the rug.

135
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:35,820
Well, it's true to him.

136
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:38,040
It's also very unfair to yourself.

137
00:10:38,620 --> 00:10:44,860
I wish every young man or woman had the good fortune I had when I was in my early 20s.

138
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:51,580
The old wise man who was the senior partner of the firm I worked for called me in and said,

139
00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:54,080
Mr. Drucker, you have been a goddamn fool again.

140
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:56,500
You have been hiding a problem.

141
00:10:56,680 --> 00:10:59,860
You have done it now twice, and if you do it a third time,

142
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:03,340
I make damn sure that you go to work for the competition.

143
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:05,620
I never did it again.

144
00:11:05,620 --> 00:11:10,690
but I needed, we had thought I was a conceited pup

145
00:11:10,690 --> 00:11:13,450
and I needed rough treatment, and I got it.

146
00:11:14,620 --> 00:11:17,700
That is a lesson most younger people need to be taught.

147
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:21,040
Their instinctive reaction is, here is a problem,

148
00:11:21,540 --> 00:11:26,280
and let's hide it because it'll go away if we hide it.

149
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:29,200
Well, maybe it will, maybe it won't,

150
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:30,620
but if it doesn't, you're in trouble.

151
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:37,170
One thinks through how one warns the boss of impending problems.

152
00:11:37,170 --> 00:11:43,900
If you work for me, you better come in and tell me what you plan to do about it.

153
00:11:44,020 --> 00:11:48,160
If you work for a friend of mine for whom I have very great respect,

154
00:11:49,070 --> 00:11:52,510
you better come in and say there is a cloud on the horizon.

155
00:11:52,890 --> 00:11:54,670
I think I know how to deal with it.

156
00:11:54,990 --> 00:11:57,410
If it gets any bigger, I'll let you know. Goodbye.

157
00:11:58,190 --> 00:12:02,700
He doesn't want to know how you propose to do your job.

158
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:05,560
He thinks that that's your business, and he may be right.

159
00:12:07,030 --> 00:12:09,470
Other people want to be part of the problem.

160
00:12:09,470 --> 00:12:12,910
where you go in and say, look, I see this coming,

161
00:12:13,390 --> 00:12:18,830
and I thought I might impose on you a time to get your advice,

162
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:22,940
to feel that after all, what the superior is there for.

163
00:12:23,320 --> 00:12:26,480
He's a coach. He's a problem shooter.

164
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:30,860
So one looks at the individual, but one makes absolutely sure

165
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:33,340
that there are no surprises.

166
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:37,720
It isn't important whether you crow about your successes

167
00:12:37,720 --> 00:12:42,700
or try to minimize your mistakes, those are fairly unimportant.

168
00:12:43,220 --> 00:12:46,880
What is terribly important is the rule, no surprises.

169
00:12:47,320 --> 00:12:52,000
It's your job to make sure your boss is never going to be surprised

170
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,860
because he has a boss too, you know.

171
00:12:54,860 --> 00:13:02,160
And his boss takes a very dim view of that sudden storm

172
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:05,140
and then you find out it's been brewing for six months

173
00:13:05,140 --> 00:13:07,090
and nobody told him about it.

174
00:13:07,170 --> 00:13:07,690
Yes, sir.

175
00:13:07,690 --> 00:13:19,190
But also if you had come in six months earlier and said,

176
00:13:19,190 --> 00:13:24,190
that account is an old customer and it looks delinquent,

177
00:13:24,190 --> 00:13:28,190
or it looks to me as if the relationship were in trouble.

178
00:13:28,190 --> 00:13:31,190
Well, maybe you can still fix it.

179
00:13:31,190 --> 00:13:35,910
Then the boss can telephone and talk to the purchasing agent

180
00:13:35,910 --> 00:13:37,910
and find out what's wrong and still fix it.

181
00:13:37,910 --> 00:13:42,910
Six months later, you have had acrimonious correspondence

182
00:13:42,910 --> 00:13:50,110
filling three file folders, and you have completely destroyed the relationship, or the account

183
00:13:50,110 --> 00:13:56,250
has become delinquent, and you now are hanging by with a bad credit, and then it's your fault.

184
00:13:56,610 --> 00:14:02,170
If you come in and say, here's that old relationship once I changed purchasing agent, and the new

185
00:14:02,170 --> 00:14:08,220
man doesn't really like us, I think the account is in danger.

186
00:14:10,250 --> 00:14:12,770
Well, that's what a boss is for, then.

187
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:14,660
He might mobilize.

188
00:14:14,660 --> 00:14:23,040
his boss who has known the chief executive officer of that customer firm for 20 years

189
00:14:23,040 --> 00:14:28,900
and takes him out for dinner or out on the golf course and finds out what is the real trouble

190
00:14:28,900 --> 00:14:34,670
or maybe tells him there's trouble and that man then can move in and say,

191
00:14:34,670 --> 00:14:38,290
well, we've been friends with these people for years, what's going on here?

192
00:14:38,710 --> 00:14:40,350
And you can still straighten it out.

193
00:14:40,410 --> 00:14:45,330
If you wait six months, you have lost that account, and indeed you have.

194
00:15:11,300 --> 00:15:17,420
I think this is crucial because you have to balance conflicting demands.

195
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:24,120
On the one hand, don't forget the very good, very sound, very wise old German peasant proverb,

196
00:15:25,580 --> 00:15:29,880
don't go near your prince unless he calls for you at least twice.

197
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:38,250
There is something to be said for not being too much in the limelight.

198
00:15:38,250 --> 00:15:45,570
On the other hand, almost every manager I know complains that the boss doesn't have enough time for him.

199
00:15:46,420 --> 00:15:47,120
And it's true.

200
00:15:47,560 --> 00:15:54,140
And yet, every manager at the same time complains that his own subordinates take too much of his time.

201
00:15:54,440 --> 00:15:55,440
And he's right, too.

202
00:15:56,040 --> 00:16:04,280
Here is that sales general manager or sales vice president of yours who has five or six who report directly to him.

203
00:16:04,280 --> 00:16:08,640
and then the market research man went another half a dozen,

204
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:12,560
all of them having a legitimate claim to his time,

205
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,880
plus, of course, people who are not in his own organization

206
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:19,240
and he has his own work to do.

207
00:16:19,380 --> 00:16:22,700
A responsible and intelligent manager

208
00:16:22,700 --> 00:16:27,920
just thinks through how to use the boss's time.

209
00:16:28,420 --> 00:16:30,340
Again, look at the man.

210
00:16:30,540 --> 00:16:32,260
You are not looking at an abstraction.

211
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:35,410
You're looking at a person, man or woman.

212
00:16:36,350 --> 00:16:42,440
Some work by sitting down for three hours with a man very rarely.

213
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:45,220
And in between, they don't want to see him.

214
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:49,080
Others prefer the ten-minute ad hoc,

215
00:16:49,930 --> 00:16:52,750
which I, for instance, consider a total waste of time

216
00:16:52,750 --> 00:16:56,280
because they don't work for me, but they work for other people.

217
00:16:56,280 --> 00:17:02,200
You have to adapt yourself to the way your boss manages his time

218
00:17:02,200 --> 00:17:03,480
or mismanages his time.

219
00:17:03,580 --> 00:17:06,220
Very little you can do about his mismanaging it.

220
00:17:06,220 --> 00:17:11,280
don't ever go into your manager's office

221
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:14,250
unless you have done your homework.

222
00:17:15,130 --> 00:17:18,610
And the old rule that it takes 10 minutes of preparation

223
00:17:18,610 --> 00:17:22,290
for each minute of interview time is a sound rule.

224
00:17:23,180 --> 00:17:33,730
You have to research into that.

225
00:17:33,790 --> 00:17:35,110
Well, don't overdo it.

226
00:17:35,110 --> 00:17:37,090
Every fact and every figure is impossible.

227
00:17:38,140 --> 00:17:40,460
More important, you have to think through

228
00:17:40,460 --> 00:17:42,300
what you expect to come out with.

229
00:17:42,780 --> 00:17:44,080
What are you going in for?

230
00:17:44,180 --> 00:17:44,880
Oh, absolutely.

231
00:17:45,260 --> 00:17:46,660
What do you want, a decision?

232
00:17:47,240 --> 00:17:48,560
You want advice.

233
00:17:49,970 --> 00:17:56,430
You want to inform the boss that down the line you might come to him with this or that.

234
00:17:56,550 --> 00:17:57,550
What are you going in for?

235
00:17:58,710 --> 00:18:00,590
Make sure the boss understands.

236
00:18:01,050 --> 00:18:03,130
Again, don't believe in your mind reading.

237
00:18:03,130 --> 00:18:08,530
Perhaps one isn't blatant, though I think the cruder the better usually.

238
00:18:08,950 --> 00:18:13,530
One doesn't go in and say, boss, this is what I'm in for here today.

239
00:18:14,190 --> 00:18:14,830
Maybe not.

240
00:18:14,910 --> 00:18:15,250
I do.

241
00:18:15,250 --> 00:18:24,880
I think it's a good idea to say, I've come to see you because I need a decision on this project.

