00:38:35,950 --> 00:38:43,360
in the top group who are your really capable you're really performing associates the list

442
00:38:43,360 --> 00:38:50,310
is never very long nobody has a simplest and then i say do you know what these people are

443
00:38:50,310 --> 00:38:58,120
assigned to and nobody knows i get the job description one goes in and finds out and one

444
00:38:58,120 --> 00:39:05,380
finds out almost without exception that the high performing people are assigned to trivia

445
00:39:05,380 --> 00:39:14,350
they are assigned to yesterday they are assigned to what is difficult they are doing interesting

446
00:39:14,350 --> 00:39:22,620
the best salesmen are rarely assigned to the most important accounts or to the new products

447
00:39:22,620 --> 00:39:39,310
They fight the rearguard action to hold a customer whom we have already lost or a customer who is himself going downhill to the product that is already obsolete and no longer really in the market.

448
00:39:39,310 --> 00:39:54,450
The same is true, particularly in research, where the most capable are then assigned to product modification so that an old product can still live on a little longer in the old age home.

449
00:39:54,450 --> 00:39:58,130
The new products are left to luck and chance.

450
00:39:58,690 --> 00:40:03,120
One looks at the assignment of one's performing people.

451
00:40:03,680 --> 00:40:09,100
There are people who have a record of getting the right things done once every six months.

452
00:40:09,700 --> 00:40:11,000
Otherwise, they drift.

453
00:40:12,010 --> 00:40:14,530
Otherwise, the trivia overwhelms them.

454
00:40:14,950 --> 00:40:23,300
Otherwise, the sheer demands of the daily operating job detracts them from performance.

455
00:40:23,300 --> 00:40:33,320
When it comes to the performer...

456
00:40:33,320 --> 00:40:41,320
I indeed do. I think the two are two very different things and lead to very different conclusions.

457
00:40:41,320 --> 00:40:51,020
One has to start out with a fairly clear idea of what performance is expected.

458
00:40:51,720 --> 00:40:55,880
The typical performance appraisal that says,

459
00:40:56,100 --> 00:41:02,500
how do you rate him from outstanding to no damn good without ever telling you what performance is, is silly.

460
00:41:02,500 --> 00:41:08,300
because there are no outstanding people, there are no poor people.

461
00:41:08,500 --> 00:41:09,520
Good for what?

462
00:41:09,840 --> 00:41:13,740
One cannot appraise performance by itself.

463
00:41:13,740 --> 00:41:21,160
One can only appraise performance against a pre-established, reasonably clear target.

464
00:41:21,420 --> 00:41:26,620
Then you say, this is what we planned to do and this is what we did,

465
00:41:26,700 --> 00:41:31,560
and in this area we did something quite different from what we had planned to do

466
00:41:31,560 --> 00:41:33,380
and it was the right thing to do.

467
00:41:33,740 --> 00:41:35,440
We learned something on the job.

468
00:41:35,440 --> 00:41:41,660
In this area, we did so much worse than we had expected to do,

469
00:41:42,330 --> 00:41:43,870
and in this one we did better.

470
00:41:44,370 --> 00:41:50,100
One learns and feeds back from a performance appraisal

471
00:41:50,100 --> 00:41:53,420
on defining performance and task again.

472
00:41:54,000 --> 00:41:55,880
But when it comes to the performer,

473
00:41:56,620 --> 00:42:01,460
one says, what has this person done well?

474
00:42:01,460 --> 00:42:10,500
I may be much too dogmatic and rigid in never permitting myself to talk about potential of people.

475
00:42:10,660 --> 00:42:14,000
I consider that obscene almost, and silly.

476
00:42:14,680 --> 00:42:24,610
I have for 35 years or so kind of watched, and there is no correlation between potential and performance.

477
00:42:24,810 --> 00:42:26,810
Believe me, neither positive nor negative.

478
00:42:26,810 --> 00:42:34,610
When you appraise a performer, you are trying to look for strength, trying to understand capacity.

479
00:42:34,730 --> 00:42:40,020
When you look for performance, you are looking for accomplishment.

480
00:42:40,360 --> 00:42:56,860
We're moving in a race.

481
00:42:56,860 --> 00:43:00,260
That depends on the audience I'm speaking to, Elizabeth.

482
00:43:00,440 --> 00:43:03,540
I would consider it one of the principal tasks.

483
00:43:04,000 --> 00:43:08,900
But if I talk to personnel people, I would not stress it

484
00:43:08,900 --> 00:43:11,220
because they are already aware of it.

485
00:43:11,620 --> 00:43:14,600
If I talk to a great many line managers,

486
00:43:14,760 --> 00:43:17,840
I'm quite likely to say it is the principal task

487
00:43:17,840 --> 00:43:21,340
because it shocks them a little bit, and they need to be shocked.

488
00:43:21,340 --> 00:43:24,500
It is one of the principal tasks,

489
00:43:24,940 --> 00:43:29,990
and it is basically the only way you can guarantee

490
00:43:29,990 --> 00:43:33,010
the perpetuation of an organization.

491
00:43:33,510 --> 00:43:37,310
Long-range planning is nice, and innovation is vital,

492
00:43:38,970 --> 00:43:42,690
but people carry out plans, and people innovate.

493
00:43:43,230 --> 00:43:45,970
And one thing you do know is that your decisions

494
00:43:45,970 --> 00:43:49,670
in respect to the future made today

495
00:43:49,670 --> 00:43:52,490
are unlikely to be the right decisions.

496
00:43:52,670 --> 00:43:53,750
Nobody knows the future.

497
00:43:54,050 --> 00:43:56,930
And then you need the people who can bail them out.

498
00:43:56,930 --> 00:44:01,930
So the development of people is one of the key tasks.

499
00:44:03,310 --> 00:44:04,990
It is being done, you know.

500
00:44:05,150 --> 00:44:08,770
No organization or no manager has a choice

501
00:44:08,770 --> 00:44:12,790
between developing people and not developing people.

502
00:44:12,790 --> 00:44:19,650
the only choice is between developing them right and misdeveloping. People develop in an

503
00:44:19,650 --> 00:44:30,960
organization because the three factors of development are the task, the climate of the

504
00:44:30,960 --> 00:44:37,840
institution, and the example set by the boss. But that is simply saying that an organization

505
00:44:37,840 --> 00:44:42,020
is a development environment, to use a big word.

506
00:44:42,480 --> 00:44:45,080
Just the way a dish of rancid butter

507
00:44:45,080 --> 00:44:48,140
is a development environment for bacteria.

508
00:44:48,870 --> 00:44:53,450
The only choice you have is whether people will develop to become bigger.

509
00:44:58,700 --> 00:45:02,040
Let me say, by the way, developing some right doesn't take more work

510
00:45:02,040 --> 00:45:04,740
and doesn't cost a penny more than developing some wrong.

511
00:45:05,140 --> 00:45:12,370
Supposedly, what do you do for him?

512
00:45:13,030 --> 00:45:15,750
With due respect, Elizabeth,

513
00:45:15,750 --> 00:45:24,290
but I would not allow a manager to say that a young man or a young woman had a real future.

514
00:45:24,810 --> 00:45:29,750
I do not believe that it is healthy for any of us to try to play providence.

515
00:45:29,950 --> 00:45:31,230
You should resist that.

516
00:45:31,230 --> 00:45:33,130
I resist it very strongly.

517
00:45:33,690 --> 00:45:42,210
I'm willing to grade a student paper, but I refuse to fill out the sheet the placement office sends me

518
00:45:42,210 --> 00:45:50,490
that asks me, in your opinion, is she likely to become a senior executive?

519
00:45:51,250 --> 00:45:55,090
I say, well, that depends on a lot of things I know nothing about,

520
00:45:55,550 --> 00:45:58,490
and not incidentally entirely on the fellow either.

521
00:45:58,850 --> 00:46:03,210
All I'm willing to say is, what is a person doing?

522
00:46:03,210 --> 00:46:08,260
I'm willing to say, on this assignment, writing up that case,

523
00:46:08,260 --> 00:46:12,920
this student did a very good job

524
00:46:12,920 --> 00:46:14,420
and I learned a lot from it.

525
00:46:14,660 --> 00:46:16,220
That's as far as I'm willing to go.

526
00:46:16,420 --> 00:46:17,700
So he gets an A+.

527
00:46:17,700 --> 00:46:21,430
But that doesn't mean anything for 10 years hence,

528
00:46:21,970 --> 00:46:24,510
though I do hope it helps him get his first job.

529
00:46:24,890 --> 00:46:27,930
Now, one looks at what people do

530
00:46:27,930 --> 00:46:33,030
and one tries to resist the temptation to play God.

531
00:46:33,030 --> 00:46:35,270
Here is that young person

532
00:46:35,270 --> 00:46:39,880
and first it's my job to give him challenge.

533
00:46:39,880 --> 00:46:46,480
People should, before they are 30, have two experiences.

534
00:46:47,770 --> 00:46:52,830
They should be able to do one piece of work

535
00:46:52,830 --> 00:46:55,750
that is really beyond them.

536
00:46:56,250 --> 00:46:58,890
That feeling of the thrill of accomplishment

537
00:46:58,890 --> 00:47:01,830
because you have done something which you know down,

538
00:47:01,910 --> 00:47:04,050
but is really beyond you.

539
00:47:04,710 --> 00:47:06,330
And they should have come a cropper.

540
00:47:07,080 --> 00:47:10,280
They should have learned that failure exists.

541
00:47:10,280 --> 00:47:13,650
They should have learned that one survives it.

542
00:47:13,650 --> 00:47:17,250
They should also have learned a little humility.

543
00:47:18,070 --> 00:47:22,670
And believe me, whom God loves, he sends failure early.

544
00:47:23,310 --> 00:47:26,490
Because if you don't have a setback until you're 45,

545
00:47:27,840 --> 00:47:29,900
you aren't going to recover from it.

546
00:47:30,000 --> 00:47:31,420
And it shatters you.

547
00:47:32,290 --> 00:47:36,490
If you have it at age 27, that broken neck heals very fast.

548
00:47:36,690 --> 00:47:38,420
No great hunger.

549
00:47:38,940 --> 00:47:43,460
So challenges is the first thing I owe that young person.

550
00:47:43,460 --> 00:47:47,320
The second thing I owe him or her is performance standards.

551
00:47:47,380 --> 00:47:52,750
I owe it to him or to her to say what should we hold you accountable for

552
00:47:52,750 --> 00:47:55,410
so that one can look at performance.

553
00:47:56,350 --> 00:48:01,230
The third thing I owe to him or to her is to look at performance,

554
00:48:01,530 --> 00:48:03,850
not in one assignment, in two or three.

555
00:48:04,110 --> 00:48:05,470
One assignment is not enough.

556
00:48:05,470 --> 00:48:10,270
And say what does this person do, what kind of attempt,

557
00:48:11,490 --> 00:48:15,630
what does he have to learn, to change, to acquire,

558
00:48:16,210 --> 00:48:18,470
to get the full benefit of his strength.

559
00:48:18,470 --> 00:48:22,530
The fourth thing, though, is placement responsibility.

560
00:48:23,230 --> 00:48:26,090
Where does he belong, including elsewhere?

561
00:48:26,770 --> 00:48:29,270
Here is a very able young person,

562
00:48:29,610 --> 00:48:34,330
and look, no matter how systematic you make the employment process,

563
00:48:34,490 --> 00:48:35,770
it's a random process.

564
00:48:36,600 --> 00:48:38,140
At the stage of employment,

565
00:48:39,240 --> 00:48:42,560
the young person knows nothing about the adult world,

566
00:48:43,000 --> 00:48:46,900
and you know nothing about him except grades in school,

567
00:48:46,900 --> 00:48:50,360
which, believe me, have no correlation to anything

568
00:48:50,360 --> 00:48:53,880
except how well you learn French irregular verbs,

569
00:48:54,080 --> 00:48:56,140
which is very useful if you want to teach them,

570
00:48:56,220 --> 00:48:57,140
but for nothing else.

571
00:48:57,440 --> 00:49:00,540
The chances that anybody is, a young person,

572
00:49:00,600 --> 00:49:03,280
is in their right employment, the right place,

573
00:49:03,460 --> 00:49:04,440
are not very good.

574
00:49:04,940 --> 00:49:06,640
Where does he really belong?

575
00:49:07,560 --> 00:49:09,420
Most of them kind of place themselves

576
00:49:09,980 --> 00:49:12,540
in a haphazard process of drifting,

577
00:49:13,080 --> 00:49:15,000
and eventually you fall into your niche.

578
00:49:15,000 --> 00:49:21,540
I think it's a very long and painful process, and I think one can and should help.

579
00:49:21,900 --> 00:49:29,300
I would probably finally ask the question with most development programs begin,

580
00:49:29,500 --> 00:49:33,240
what kind of courses should, so on.

581
00:49:33,280 --> 00:49:39,450
This, I think, comes at the very end, and it's a supplement, a very useful dose of vitamins,

582
00:49:39,950 --> 00:49:41,310
but it isn't the main dish.

583
00:49:41,940 --> 00:49:48,880
But I think that the effective managers and the development people

584
00:49:48,880 --> 00:49:51,300
as their tool to build an organization.

585
00:49:51,300 --> 00:49:54,940
And it is not very difficult. It does not take a lot of time.

586
00:49:55,060 --> 00:50:06,030
It seems development.

587
00:50:06,670 --> 00:50:09,970
It is perfectly true that a manager is always an example.

588
00:50:10,610 --> 00:50:15,140
But people are perhaps a little more resistant than that.

589
00:50:15,520 --> 00:50:20,680
I think that one organizes the few development decisions.

590
00:50:21,300 --> 00:50:23,280
And it doesn't worry too much about the rest.

591
00:50:23,420 --> 00:50:27,680
The first is the assignment. What is the job?

592
00:50:27,680 --> 00:50:33,480
That is not only the test of performance, but also the learning situation.

593
00:50:33,720 --> 00:50:39,720
What kind of a boss should this person have to the extent to which one can control it?

594
00:50:39,980 --> 00:50:46,140
Particularly if I think highly of a young person, I would never put him to work under a boss

595
00:50:46,140 --> 00:50:52,620
unless I would be willing to have him model himself or herself on that boss.

596
00:50:52,900 --> 00:50:54,140
Bosses are examples.

597
00:50:54,140 --> 00:51:14,300
Finally, one says, what are the strengths of a person that he really needs to develop and where does he belong so that his strengths can make the greatest contribution to him and to this world of ours, where he can be the most productive person?

598
00:51:14,660 --> 00:51:18,480
Maybe that's my department, but maybe it isn't.

599
00:51:18,640 --> 00:51:20,400
And then, well, I'm older.

600
00:51:20,540 --> 00:51:21,580
I know the organization.

601
00:51:21,920 --> 00:51:25,140
I can talk to the colleague of mine in accounting

602
00:51:25,140 --> 00:51:27,260
and say, Jones, I think,

603
00:51:27,360 --> 00:51:30,860
should have some years of accounting experience.

604
00:51:31,260 --> 00:51:33,800
That's where he really has the strength.

605
00:51:33,800 --> 00:51:37,670
But I can also say to Jones, we like you here.

606
00:51:37,750 --> 00:51:39,130
You are doing good work.

607
00:51:39,900 --> 00:51:42,340
But this is the wrong place for you.

608
00:51:42,480 --> 00:51:46,140
This is a people-focused, shooting-from-the-hip,

609
00:51:46,300 --> 00:51:53,980
intuitive organization and you are an analyst and conceptually oriented we never really put it to

610
00:51:53,980 --> 00:52:01,790
work and really respect this isn't the business we are in or you are really only interested and

611
00:52:01,790 --> 00:52:08,230
your strengths lies in a very small specialty and you want to perfect this and be the world's

612
00:52:08,230 --> 00:52:17,340
greatest expert on heat coupling whatever this may be well that's a very minor point really we

613
00:52:17,340 --> 00:52:24,140
don't require much more than the draftsman's. Where are you going to go? In this organization,

614
00:52:24,320 --> 00:52:32,100
you are going to remain an overpaid draftsman. Here are the businesses where you're particularly

615
00:52:32,100 --> 00:52:42,380
needed, and they'll help you get. One does this on an organized basis, and then one doesn't have

616
00:52:42,380 --> 00:52:49,550
to worry too much about every organization in which it worries so much about human relations.

617
00:52:49,550 --> 00:52:56,280
Always organizations in which human relations are poor because their basic job of managing

618
00:52:56,280 --> 00:53:03,520
people isn't done organizations which do a job of managing people don't worry about polite

619
00:53:03,520 --> 00:53:15,970
about advances and participation and all together are not terribly self-conscious just as if you

620
00:53:15,970 --> 00:53:21,330
really have a good friend you are not terribly worried about hurting him he's secure in their

621
00:53:21,330 --> 00:53:27,910
relationship and good marriages are usually very noisy marriages if you are self-conscious

622
00:53:27,910 --> 00:53:49,400
You're unlikely to do a good job, but you're also unlikely to have good human activities, anything.

623
00:53:49,840 --> 00:53:59,540
Any organization, like any organism, has to have a process of elimination or else the toxic waste poisons the whole system.

624
00:53:59,540 --> 00:54:21,130
Any manager needs to set up his own system for putting every product, every service, every activity, every procedure, every carbon copy on trial for its life every two, three years and say, if we didn't do it, would we now go into it?

625
00:54:21,130 --> 00:54:25,790
If the answer is no, one says, how do we get out of it, and does it fast.

626
00:54:26,030 --> 00:54:30,510
Procedures in particular outlive their usefulness amazingly.

627
00:54:31,530 --> 00:54:33,450
They become sheer business.

628
00:54:34,530 --> 00:54:40,670
And so to organize abandonment is, I think, an essential management tool

629
00:54:40,670 --> 00:54:44,330
without which the organization won't stay healthy.

630
00:54:44,810 --> 00:54:48,950
It's, so to speak, the hygiene of an organization

631
00:54:48,950 --> 00:54:56,700
and of a managerial group that's closely tied in with assignment control

632
00:54:56,700 --> 00:55:02,300
because usually you find where the candidates for abandonment are

633
00:55:02,300 --> 00:55:06,620
when you look at the assignment of your performing people

634
00:55:06,620 --> 00:55:09,680
and realize that the stronger they are,

635
00:55:10,220 --> 00:55:16,440
the more likely they are to be assigned to the areas where there are no results.

636
00:55:16,440 --> 00:55:23,280
you then identify the areas where you either abandon or prune back sharp.

637
00:55:23,420 --> 00:55:28,070
I think this is essential hygiene, and if you don't do it,

638
00:55:28,230 --> 00:55:32,090
you are likely to become fat and flabby and unhealthy.

639
00:55:33,180 --> 00:55:41,700
Peter, do you want to call it that?

640
00:55:41,900 --> 00:55:51,560
We have been talking about very specific things that are characteristic of the task of the manager

641
00:55:51,560 --> 00:55:57,500
in the way in which the pliers or the chisel pertain to the craft of the carpenter.

642
00:55:57,980 --> 00:56:01,160
There is nothing philosophical about the pair of pliers,

643
00:56:01,720 --> 00:56:05,540
but without it, a carpenter can't do his job.

644
00:56:05,780 --> 00:56:10,340
There is nothing very philosophical, I think, about the things we have been talking about,

645
00:56:10,340 --> 00:56:14,940
but without them, a manager is unlikely to do his job.

646
00:56:15,840 --> 00:56:20,980
Managers better realize that they depend on these tools

647
00:56:20,980 --> 00:56:27,020
and that they better learn how to use them effectively and effortlessly.
